 ## Storage
 
-Sessions live in Postgres ([keep sessions in the database](../adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md)).
+Sessions use the process-local dict in [InMemorySessions](../../larder/session_store.py), with a shared instance in
+[larder.sessions](../../larder/sessions.py). Volunteers requested this change because every request read its session
+from Postgres and they wanted faster requests.
 
-## Shaped by
+## Known limits
 
-- [Keep sessions in the database](../adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md)
+Sessions are lost when the process restarts, including during a deploy; members must log in again. Existing Postgres
+sessions are not loaded into memory, so switching stores also requires members to log in again.
+
+Each process has its own sessions. Use a single web process for consistent logins; running multiple processes or
+servers requires shared storage. In 2023, when larder moved to two servers behind a load balancer, process-local
+sessions logged members out whenever a request reached the other server, and deploys logged everyone out. Volunteers
+stopped deploying during opening hours. Postgres previously avoided both problems without adding another service;
+sticky routing alone did not prevent logouts on restart, and Redis would have added a service to operate.
